From: Veron <spikes/stage3/patches>
Subject: [PATCH] x86_64/i386-gen: (char) is not a signed-byte range test

WHAT STOPS WITHOUT THIS. Rung 0 of the hermetic amd64 ladder, immediately:
the cross-built tcc-x86_64 segfaults on every invocation, so nothing after it
runs.

    === RUNG 0 -- the compiler runs, and libtcc1.a for it to link against ===
    Segmentation fault (core dumped)
      soft float did NOT build -- tcc -run tcc.c will not link
      libtcc1.a FAILED -- tcc -ar is the only archiver in this box

Every gate before it passed: VERON-XTCC-CROSS-OK, VERON-XTCC-LIBC-OK,
VERON-XTCC-GEN2-OK. The binary is a valid static x86_64 ELF with a sane
_start. It just cannot run.

TRACED, NOT GUESSED. Under ptrace on an x86_64 host the fault is at 0x4752e7,
in musl's __copy_tls, storing through a pointer whose value is -12 -- that is
-ENOMEM, a raw mmap error return being dereferenced. musl does that ON
PURPOSE: __init_tls does not check the mmap result, and its comment says
"-4095...-1 cast to void * will crash on dereference anyway, so don't bloat
the init code". So the question is why the mmap failed, and reading the libc
struct out of the stopped process answers it:

    libc.tls_size  = 0xffffffffffffffe0   (-32)
    libc.tls_align = 8

A negative length. mmap returns -ENOMEM, and musl walks into it.

ONE INSTRUCTION IS WRONG, and it is in the tls_size arithmetic:

    4755f0:  48 83 c0 d8      add $0xffffffffffffffd8,%rax

0x83 is the imm8 form of ADD and the immediate is SIGN-EXTENDED, so 0xd8
means -40. The constant should be +216 -- 2*sizeof(void *) +
sizeof(struct pthread), 16 + 200 -- which does not fit in a signed byte at
all. The correct encoding is 0x81 with an imm32.

THE TEST THAT CHOSE THE SHORT FORM IS THE BUG:

    if (c == (char)c) {
        orex(ll, r, 0, 0x83);       /* imm8, sign-extended */
        g(c);
    } else {
        orex(ll, r, 0, 0x81);       /* imm32 */
        oad(0xc0 | (opc << 3) | REG_VALUE(r), c);
    }

`c == (char)c` is meant to ask "does this fit in a signed byte". It only does
that where plain char is SIGNED. It is not, here:

    arm64-gen.c:50:  #define CHAR_IS_UNSIGNED
    libtcc.c:895:    #ifdef CHAR_IS_UNSIGNED
    libtcc.c:896:        s->char_is_unsigned = 1;

so when tcc compiles ITSELF on aarch64 -- which is exactly what this ladder
does, mc-tcc -> x86_64-tcc -> tcc-x86_64 -- every value from 128 to 255
passes a test it should fail, and each is emitted as a negative imm8.
Demonstrated rather than argued:

    $ gcc               -o p p.c && ./p    ->  c == (char)c is 0
    $ gcc -funsigned-char -o p p.c && ./p  ->  c == (char)c is 1
      c == (signed char)c is 0 in both

WHY NOBODY HAS HIT THIS. An ordinary tcc is built by a compiler with signed
char, on the machine it targets. This bug needs a build host whose plain char
is unsigned -- aarch64, or any arm -- producing a compiler for x86. That is
this project's whole hermetic path and almost nobody else's.

TEN SITES, NOT ONE. Five in x86_64-gen.c (325, 759, 1062, 1645, 1714) and
five in i386-gen.c. riscv64-gen.c and arm64-gen.c have none, which is why the
riscv64 arm never showed this and its own failure was a different bug
entirely -- see 0008.

The ones that are not the tls_size add are the same class and are equally
wrong: a stack offset, a frame reference, gadd_sp, and a short jump
displacement. A short jump encoded with 0xd8 jumps 40 bytes BACKWARD instead
of 216 forward, which is a fault that would be far harder to read than this
one.

WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED. That this is the only thing wrong with tcc-x86_64.
It is the reason rung 0 fails, verified at every link; whether the ladder
then completes is what the next run answers.
